aept (0.5.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Change: Recommends are no longer installed. libsolv satisfies them by
    default, which is apt's behaviour and the wrong one for a small root:
    a package pulled in whatever its recommends named, transitively, and
    nothing chose that, documented it, or could switch it off. Set
    "option install_recommends 1" for apt's behaviour. Suggests were never
    followed and still are not.
  * Change: "Conflicts: X" with "Replaces: X" no longer makes a package an
    update candidate for X. libsolv's Debian reader files Replaces under
    obsoletes, and to libsolv an obsoletes is a supersede -- so every mail
    transport agent was an upgrade path for every other, and a plain
    "aept upgrade" could swap one for another nobody asked for. The pair
    now means what Debian says it means: X must go for this package to be
    installed, and this package may take X's files on the way. That
    ordering -- install over, then remove what is left -- is aept's own,
    since libsolv orders a conflict's removal first.
  * New: "Replaces: X" on its own -- the file-move form, normally paired
    with a versioned Breaks on a package split -- is honoured rather than
    refused. Both packages stay installed, the path leaves X's file list
    so removing X later does not delete it, and a package left owning no
    files at all disappears: its postrm is called with "disappear
    <overwriter> <version>" and its records are cleared. Breaks is not
    read as a conflict for this purpose; a versioned Breaks is satisfied
    by moving the other package on, as the field asks.
  * New: aept parses package indexes itself, through libsolv's own
    solvable API, instead of calling libsolvext's Debian reader. That
    reader is what folded Replaces into obsoletes, and owning the parse
    is what lets Replaces and Conflicts be kept as the packager wrote
    them. It accepts the binary-package grammar -- names, version
    relations, alternatives -- and refuses what it cannot represent,
    dropping the package rather than silently weakening a dependency.
    Verified field-for-field against the archive index and against
    Debian trixie's 68,825 packages. libsolvext is no longer needed to
    build or run aept.
  * Fix: version comparison followed whatever libsolv had been built for.
    Debian's package chooses Debian semantics; an upstream cmake build
    defaults to RPM's, which orders 726 of 23,259 real Debian version
    pairs differently -- "1.0.1-1" against "1.0+2-1" is an upgrade to one
    and a downgrade to the other. aept now asks for Debian semantics and
    refuses to start against a libsolv that cannot provide them.
  * Fix: "aept mark manual --all" died in the subcommand's option parser
    on glibc and worked on musl. Dispatching parsers stop at the first
    non-option and leaf parsers permute; both reset with optind = 0.
  * Fix: the signal handler could read a context that aept_cleanup() had
    already freed.
  * Fix: aept_architectures() returned -1 with no error recorded, and
    handed back an array with a NULL in it when strdup() failed. api.c
    now uses the allocators that cannot fail.
  * Fix: aept show reported Installed-Size in bytes as kB, printed the
    description twice, and named fields as libsolv had rendered them.
    It now prints the candidate version (every version under -a), reads
    the relationship fields from the control stanza as the packager
    wrote them, and adds Section, Source, Maintainer and Download-Size.
    aept list agrees with install on which version is the candidate.
  * Fix: a download that succeeded erased the classification of one that
    had failed earlier in the same call, so an update that lost a source
    to a timeout reported nothing in particular. aept_last_error() is
    now cleared once, when a call is entered, and set only by failures.
    An update that lost any source reports AEPT_ERR_GENERAL rather than
    passing up a per-source timeout that may have happened a successful
    source ago, and whose choice depended on the order of the sources.
  * New: libaept no longer ends its embedder's process on allocation
    failure. A failed allocation unwinds to the public entry point, which
    returns -1 with AEPT_ERR_NOMEM; the Python bindings raise
    AeptNoMemory. Partial state is not rolled back, only the process
    survives.
  * New: "option clean_cache 0" makes clean leave the cache directory
    alone, for a cache shared with something outside the root.
  * New: "aept show" takes -a/--all to print every offered version.
  * Tests: every relationship field has a contract test, every public
    entry point is swept for allocation and I/O failure at every point,
    and the suite runs under musl in a container (scripts/musl-build.sh),
    which is how the version-comparison bug was found. The package build
    runs the format and coverage gates.
  * ABI: libaept goes from 0:3:0 to 1:0:0 (libaept1). aept_set_offline_root()
    and aept_set_cache_dir() return int, being the only setters that can
    fail; aept_pkg_info_t gained section, source, maintainer and
    download_size; aept_show_all(), aept_pkg_info_list_t and
    AEPT_ERR_NOMEM are added. The Python bindings expose the new fields,
    show_all(), and AeptNoMemory.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 11:40:29 +0300

aept (0.4.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: the connection cache never engaged against an HTTP/1.1 server
    that says nothing about persistence -- which is what nginx and
    Caddy do, since RFC 9112 9.3 makes persistence the default and a
    server signals it by staying silent. libfetch started from
    HTTP/1.0's rule and turned reuse on only for an explicit
    Connection: keep-alive, so every index and every package paid a
    fresh name lookup and a fresh TLS handshake. The default now comes
    from the reply's version. Measured over five downloads of one URL:
    10 connect() calls and 5 DNS queries before, 2 and 1 after.
  * Fix: Connection is a token list, so "keep-alive, TE" and "TE,
    close" are both legal and neither was recognised by comparing the
    whole field value against one token. It is parsed as a list now,
    with close winning when a peer names both.
  * No ABI or Python API change; libaept stays at 0:3:0.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Fri, 04 Sep 2026 19:20:41 +0300

aept (0.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New: the cache directory can now also be set via the AEPT_CACHE_DIR
    environment variable, in addition to -C/--cache-dir on the command
    line. Precedence: --cache-dir > AEPT_CACHE_DIR > option cache_dir >
    default. This lets a wrapper set the override once and have it
    apply to aept invocations it does not launch itself -- e.g.
    bootstrap scripts run against an offline root.
  * No ABI or Python API change; libaept stays at 0:3:0.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Fri, 04 Sep 2026 10:28:06 +0300

aept (0.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Packaging: the 0.3.0 release left configure.ac at 0.2.0, so the
    version check in debian/rules refused the build and 0.3.0 never
    produced a package -- which is the check doing its job, since the
    binary would otherwise have reported aept/0.2.0 as its User-Agent
    to every repository it fetched from. configure.ac and
    python/pyproject.toml now carry the release version. No source
    change otherwise; libaept stays at 0:3:0, which no release ever
    shipped.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Fri, 04 Sep 2026 09:07:44 +0300

aept (0.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New: -C/--cache-dir global option overrides the cache directory
    verbatim. Unlike option cache_dir in the config file, the CLI value
    is treated as a host path and is not prefixed with the offline
    root -- the "CLI is literal" rule, documented in the manual.
  * New: libaept 0:3:0 - aept_set_cache_dir() added, nothing removed
    or changed; existing consumers keep working unrebuilt. Python
    bindings gain a matching set_cache_dir() method.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Fri, 04 Sep 2026 01:24:38 +0300

aept (0.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New: failed trigger scripts are persisted and retried. The owed
    directories are recorded per package before the script runs, the
    package status is set to triggers-pending, and every later
    transaction (or the new "aept triggers" command) retries until the
    script succeeds. A transaction that succeeds but leaves a failed
    trigger exits 2, distinct from success (0) and failure (1).
  * New: libaept 0:2:0 - aept_triggers() and AEPT_ERR_TRIGGER added,
    nothing removed or changed; existing consumers keep working
    unrebuilt. Python bindings updated accordingly; transaction methods
    now return False when a trigger is owed.
  * Changed: installing an older package file now requires
    --allow-downgrade, matching the by-name behaviour. Previously an
    explicit local file downgraded silently.
  * Changed: credentials in source URLs are split off at config-parse
    time and reunited only when the request is built. They no longer
    appear in any log message or state file, and a raw '@' in a
    password now works.
  * Fixed: a rejected index update (bad signature, tampered envelope)
    no longer destroys the previously verified index. The new index is
    verified aside and renamed into place only on success.
  * Fixed: a segfault when installing a package by name while a version
    pin for it existed.
  * Fixed: the manual page no longer documents the removed
    triggers-index file, and documents the new trigger failure
    semantics, exit status 2, and the triggers command.
  * Packaging: usign added to Build-Depends (test-only, <!nocheck>);
    debian/copyright now enumerates the BSD-licensed libfetch fork;
    NOTICE carries the OpenSSL license for statically linked builds.
  * Tests: suite grown from 26 to 49 tests, covering the full
    update-install-verify chain, transactions, triggers, pins, the
    public API, the Python bindings, TLS rejection and the CLI. Line
    coverage 57% -> 83%.

 -- Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@gmail.com>  Wed, 19 Aug 2026 17:25:20 +0300

